WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:12.627
Quarkus, Spring and Hibernate add behavior with proxies: subclasses they generate, which override your methods to run code before and after them.

2
00:00:12.833 --> 00:00:18.730
But Kotlin classes and methods are final unless you write open, so the subclass can't exist.

3
00:00:18.730 --> 00:00:21.551
And nobody keeps writing open on every bean.

4
00:00:22.433 --> 00:00:27.517
A compiler plugin is a jar that kotlinc loads, to change what it emits.

5
00:00:27.733 --> 00:00:33.595
all-open opens each class that carries an annotation you configure, and its members.

6
00:00:33.595 --> 00:00:35.619
Here, that is OpenForProxies.

7
00:00:35.800 --> 00:00:39.799
javap on the output shows public class, with no final.

8
00:00:39.799 --> 00:00:41.872
Now a proxy can subclass it.

9
00:00:42.067 --> 00:00:47.215
Private members, backing fields, and classes without the annotation stay as they were.

10
00:00:47.433 --> 00:00:52.261
kotlin-spring is the same plugin, with Spring's annotations already listed.

11
00:00:52.533 --> 00:00:54.485
Hibernate needs something else.

12
00:00:54.485 --> 00:01:00.717
It builds an entity with a constructor that takes no parameters, then sets the fields from the row.

13
00:01:00.717 --> 00:01:04.180
A Kotlin class with val id in its constructor has none.

14
00:01:04.367 --> 00:01:07.686
no-arg adds that constructor to the classes you mark.

15
00:01:07.686 --> 00:01:11.505
It's hidden from Kotlin, but Java and reflection can call it.

16
00:01:11.700 --> 00:01:14.860
The catch: it doesn't run your property initializers.

17
00:01:14.860 --> 00:01:18.497
So note is null, though Kotlin declared it a non-null String.

18
00:01:18.700 --> 00:01:22.725
For an entity, Hibernate fills the mapped columns right after.

19
00:01:22.725 --> 00:01:25.711
For anything else, turn on invokeInitializers.

20
00:01:25.967 --> 00:01:33.568
Most JSON libraries on the JVM use reflection: at run time they look at the class and guess how to fill it.

21
00:01:33.800 --> 00:01:36.308
kotlinx.serialization doesn't guess.

22
00:01:36.308 --> 00:01:45.227
For each @Serializable class, the plugin writes a serializer class at compile time, with real serialize and deserialize methods.

23
00:01:46.400 --> 00:01:47.075
The rule.

24
00:01:47.075 --> 00:01:52.322
A compiler plugin changes what kotlinc emits for the classes you mark.

25
00:01:52.322 --> 00:01:59.369
all-open removes final, no-arg adds an empty constructor, and serialization adds a serializer.

26
00:01:59.567 --> 00:02:00.643
Quick check.

27
00:02:00.643 --> 00:02:07.457
Kotlin 2.4.21's quarkus preset lists the javax ApplicationScoped annotation.

28
00:02:07.457 --> 00:02:11.402
Your Quarkus 3 bean carries the jakarta one.

29
00:02:11.402 --> 00:02:12.927
Is the bean open?

30
00:02:15.300 --> 00:02:15.517
No.

31
00:02:15.517 --> 00:02:20.011
The jakarta name isn't on that list, so the class stays final.

32
00:02:20.011 --> 00:02:22.765
List the jakarta annotations yourself.
